BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post #4 — The bigger why (research framing)
DTSTART:20260727T150000Z
DTEND:20260727T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260726T220421Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-4@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:POST 4 — The bigger why (research framing)\n\nPost text:\nHo
 ney bees have lost 30–50% of managed colonies most years since the mid-2
 000s. Most of the survivors only make it because of constant human interve
 ntion.\n\nThe colonies that make it on their own — no treatment\, no hel
 p — are living proof that natural resistance is possible. Finding and tr
 acking them is the whole point of SaveTheHives. Every pin on our map is a 
 lead worth following. savethehives.org\n\nImage: images/learn-hero.jpg (sa
 me bee-line/tree illustration set\, alternate crop — keeps visual consis
 tency with Post 2 without literally repeating it)\n\nAlt text: "Illustrati
 on of a honey bee in a wildflower meadow with a hollow tree in the distanc
 e."\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as-is — no preview card will generate
  since a photo is attached.\n\nSuggested timing: week 2\, day 2–3\n\nNot
 e: the 30–50% loss-rate figure is a general\, widely-cited industry rang
 e\, not SaveTheHives-collected data — fine for casual Facebook use\, but
  add a source if this copy ever moves to a press pitch.\n\nFull copy in FA
 CEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §3.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Triangulation (first ongoing Wed post)
DTSTART:20260729T150000Z
DTEND:20260729T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260726T220421Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-triangulation-20260729@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot\n"You
  caught the line — now what?" (Triangulation)\n\nPost text:\nBeelining g
 ets you a direction. Triangulation gets you the tree.\n\nOnce you've fed a
  bee and watched which way she flies home\, walk a few hundred feet to the
  side and feed another bee from the same patch. She'll fly a different lin
 e back to the same colony. Follow both lines out\, and the point where the
 y'd cross is roughly where the nest is — two data points beat one guess 
 every time.\n\nIt's the same technique behind a lot of the historical reco
 rds already on our map. Full walkthrough with diagrams is in the app's Lea
 rn tab. savethehives.org/?tab=learn\n\nImage: images/learn-triangulation.j
 pg (Learn tab Module 3 illustration — real asset in repo)\n\nAlt text: "
 Diagram illustrating two bee flight lines crossing at a hollow tree\, show
 ing the triangulation technique."\n\nLink: use savethehives.org/?tab=learn
  as trailing plain text — a photo is attached so no Facebook link-previe
 w card will generate regardless.\n\nSuggested timing: Wed Jul 29\, 11am–
 2pm ET\n\nNote: first ongoing-rotation Wednesday post (launch sequence's 5
  posts are used up as of Post 5 / Aug 2). New content\, not in FACEBOOK_ST
 ARTER_PACK.md — generated this run per §5 Wednesday practical/recruitin
 g slot.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
