BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Why do queens mate so much? (research hoo
 k)
DTSTART:20260803T150000Z
DTEND:20260803T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260726T220421Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-queenmating-20260803@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot\n"Why do quee
 ns mate so much?" — RESERVED research hook\, pre-verified (do not resubs
 titute a fresh search this week)\n\nExpert-authorship check: PASSED — al
 l three authors (Tarpy\, Delaney\, Seeley) are in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md. 
 Dr. Tarpy is our confirmed scientific advisor\; Dr. Delaney was emailed 20
 26-07-25 (awaiting reply)\; Dr. Seeley is a lead in the University Researc
 h Program Contacts list. Lean on this in the post copy.\n\nPost text:\nTur
 ns out one of honey bees' strangest traits might be a survival trick. Quee
 ns mate with 10–20+ drones each — far more than needed just for geneti
 c diversity. A 2015 study by Dr. David Tarpy\, Dr. Debbie Delaney\, and Dr
 . Thomas Seeley (our own scientific advisor among them) genotyped offsprin
 g from feral colonies in NY's Arnot Forest and found their queens mate at 
 the same high rate as managed-colony queens — no more\, no less. That po
 ints to something interesting: this level of polyandry likely evolved over
  deep evolutionary time\, not as a recent adaptation to survive Varroa. It
 's baked in. Every non-managed colony we map carries that same trait — o
 ne more reason each one is worth finding. savethehives.org\n\nImage: no as
 set fits yet — consider a fresh photo\, or fall back to images/honeybee-
 on-comb.jpg (already used for Post 1 — some repeat risk)\n\nAlt text: se
 t based on whichever image is used\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as plain
  text if a photo is attached — no preview card will generate\n\nSource (
 verified Jul 22 2026\, primary source checked directly): Tarpy DR\, Delane
 y DA\, Seeley TD (2015). "Mating Frequencies of Honey Bee Queens (Apis mel
 lifera L.) in a Population of Feral Colonies in the Northeastern United St
 ates." PLOS ONE 10(3). https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.137
 1/journal.pone.0118734\n\nNote: a companion paper by the same three author
 s (Seeley\, Tarpy\, Griffin\, Carcione\, Delaney 2015\, Apidologie 46:654-
 666) is a good follow-up a few weeks out — don't use back-to-back.\n\nSu
 ggested timing: Mon Aug 3\, 11am–2pm ET\n\nFull copy in FACEBOOK_STARTER
 _PACK.md §4.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Not every nest is a honey bee colony (bee
  ID)
DTSTART:20260805T150000Z
DTEND:20260805T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260726T220421Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-beeid-20260805@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot\n"Not
  every buzzing nest is a honey bee colony" (bee ID / logging accuracy)\n\n
 Post text:\nNot every buzzing nest you find is a honey bee colony — and 
 that's fine\, it happens to everyone.\n\nHoney bees build vertical wax com
 b inside a cavity (a hollow tree\, wall void\, or bee box)\, and you'll us
 ually see a steady stream of bees flying in and out\, even on cool days. B
 umblebees and yellowjackets nest in small ground colonies or paper nests a
 nd don't look anything like that. If you're not sure what you found\, log 
 it anyway — a photo is enough for us to help sort it out\, and every sub
 mission\, even a maybe\, helps keep the map honest.\n\nsavethehives.org\n\
 nImage: images/learn-bee-box.jpg (Learn tab reference asset showing what a
  colony/hive looks like)\n\nAlt text: "Illustration of a honey bee hive bo
 x used as a reference for identifying wild colonies."\n\nLink: keep the tr
 ailing URL as plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will g
 enerate.\n\nSuggested timing: Wed Aug 5\, 11am–2pm ET\n\nNote: second on
 going-rotation Wednesday post. New content\, not in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.
 md — generated this run per §5 Wednesday practical/recruiting slot (bee
  ID / safety angle called out explicitly in §5).
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — How this started (origin story)
DTSTART:20260809T150000Z
DTEND:20260809T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260726T220421Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-howthisstarted-20260809@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot\n"How th
 is started" (ready post from FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4\, added Jul 22 2
 026)\n\nPost text:\nBefore this was an app\, it was a friendship. SaveTheH
 ives actually started in 2008 — Ronnie was an active beekeeper and met D
 r. David Tarpy at an NC State beekeepers meeting. One thing led to another
 \, and the two started "The Feral Bee Project": researchers needed known l
 ocations of wild honey bee colonies for genetic sampling tied to Colony Co
 llapse Disorder research\, so we built a simple web map for anyone to log 
 what they found. By 2012 there was even an iPhone app (Honey Bee Mapper). 
 The project got cited in outside bee research and grew entries from around
  the world before the original site went dark around 2015.\n\nThose origin
 al 1\,000+ records never disappeared — they're the backbone of the map y
 ou see today\, and Dr. Tarpy is back on board as scientific advisor. Same 
 mission\, 18 years later. savethehives.org\n\nImage: no asset fits yet —
  consider a fresh photo\, or SaveTheHives_map_screenshot_cropped.jpg if no
 thing else is available (repeat risk: already used for Post 3)\n\nAlt text
 : set based on whichever image is used\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as p
 lain text if a photo is attached — no preview card will generate\n\nSour
 ce: full origin story in PROJECT_HISTORY.md (Era 0)\n\nSuggested timing: S
 un Aug 9\, 11am–2pm ET\n\nFull copy in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
